 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "5"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "7"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "8"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "9"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->\
"1fa716df-526a-cf45-be58-73d37bff5388"],

Cell["7. Scope and limits", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d904e9de-cb0b-5642-b277-e136365e6351"],

Cell["\<\
The evolution is exhaustive: every rule in every way it fits, on every state \
reached. That is what a multiway system is, and it is also how it gets \
expensive. The number of states can grow faster than expected, so \"MaxStates\
\" \[LongDash] 2048 by default \[LongDash] stops the run and says so rather \
than going on.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b790585a-259f-e748-a2a8-c953a5dc5aa7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "A rule holds the symbols it was written with, including the ones serving as \
label variables. Giving one of those symbols a value later turns every copy \
of the rule into whatever the value is, and an expression holding itself is \
not a pleasant thing to evaluate. Keeping the variables of a rule clear of \
assignments is not a style preference here; x = something after the fact will \
break rules written months earlier."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"4c03ee92-4152-ae43-9df3-d4fac5bec7d0"],

Cell["\<\
What is not here: the branchial structure beyond a single generation, causal \
invariance as a test rather than something to look at, and the token-event \
graph in the form the Multicomputation paclet gives it. Those are all built \
out of what is above and none of them needs a new idea.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"020e51cc-c21c-1243-b4be-5370706b7908"],

Cell["\<\
The syntax follows the reference paclets: where they write \
MultiwaySystem[rules, init][\"CausalGraph\", n], this writes \
HypergraphRewritingSystem[rules, init][\"CausalGraph\", n], with the same \
property names. What differs is the option: their causal dependence is fixed \
at the weakest reading, and here it is chosen.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0b49d653-838a-2d4a-a861-2a723bec6422"],

Cell["\<\
The file tests/CausalityExamples.wls runs the examples in this notebook as \
assertions, plus invariants checked over a hundred and twenty generated \
systems: that a causal edge always runs from an earlier generation to a later \
one, that it never reports an overlap it does not have, and that the three \
readings stand in the relation section 4 claims.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e734e3ff-02dc-9a48-89d4-911a50b44727"],

Cell["\<\
Next: the plex diagrams that tie rewriting to hypermatrix algebra.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a73ab8a5-dc09-4244-bc70-10d7e1ea95ab"]
},
InitializationCellEvaluation->True,
InitializationCellWarning->False,
